course: dictionary
lesson: 12677
title: Distortion: when things are twisted
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A distortion is a twisting or stretching of something's shape, sound or picture, so it looks or sounds wrong. The word comes from the verb distort, which means to twist out of shape. «The funhouse mirror made my head look tiny and my legs look huge.» The mirror did not change you, only the picture, so that is a distortion.
explain: The word also works for facts. «The advert showed only the good parts, which was a distortion of the truth.» Nothing false was said, but the picture of the product was bent so people got the wrong idea. Ask yourself: does this make the real thing look different from how it really is?
explain: Sound can be distorted too. «The phone call was crackly and her voice sounded like a robot.» Her voice was twisted in sound, so it was distorted. In music, a rough buzzing sound on an electric guitar is also called distortion, and there it is used on purpose.
fact: Distortion means a twisting or stretching of shape, sound or picture, so that it looks or sounds wrong.
fact: Distort is the verb: a curved mirror can distort your face.
fact: A distortion of the truth or facts is a version that gives a false idea.
fact: In music, distortion is a rough, buzzing sound effect used on electric guitars.
fact: In science, lenses and water can cause distortion of light: a straw in a glass of water looks bent.
exercise: «The funhouse mirror made my head look tiny.» What is the distortion here? | The room is very dark | My head has really shrunk | *The picture of my head is twisted, so it looks wrong | The mirror is painted red
exercise: «The advert showed only the good parts.» Why is this a distortion of the truth? | The advert is very long | It is shown on television | *It gives a false idea of the product | It uses bright colours
exercise: What is distortion in music? | *A rough, buzzing sound used on electric guitars | A song sung in a different language | A quiet, soft tune played on a piano
test: «The straw in the glass of water looked bent.» What causes this? | Someone bent it on purpose | The straw is made of bendy plastic | The glass is cracked | *Distortion of light passing through water
test: «The newspaper cut off the numbers on the chart, which was a distortion of the facts.» What is a distortion of facts? | A true report with every number shown | A newspaper with no pictures | *A version that gives a false idea | A chart with numbers in colour
exam: Which sentence shows a distortion of sound? | The bus was late this morning | He ate three apples | The garden was full of red roses | *The phone call made her voice crackly and robotic
